import { buildArticleSchema } from '../../lib/schema';
const title = 'Changing Packaging Artwork Without Mixing Old and New Stock';
const description = 'Plan a packaging artwork changeover with stock run-out calculations, revision labels, receiving checks and approval gates for Canadian and US buyers.';
const image = '/images/blog/2026-11-26/artwork-changeover-hero.webp';
const articleSchema = { ...buildArticleSchema({ title, description, url: '/blog/packaging-artwork-changeover-stock-plan', datePublished: '2026-11-26', author: 'Apex Packaging Solutions' }), image: `https://apexpackagingsolutions.com${image}` };
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} image={image} schemaBlocks={[articleSchema]}>
<header class="border-b border-neutral-200 bg-[#f7f2e8] py-10 md:py-16"><div class="container max-w-wide grid items-center gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2"><div><p class="eyebrow">Packaging operations · <time datetime="2026-11-26">November 26, 2026</time></p><h1 class="mt-4 font-display text-4xl font-extrabold leading-tight md:text-5xl">{title}</h1><p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-neutral-600">Set the switching rule, reconcile committed stock and release the right revision to the packing line.</p></div><figure><img src={image} alt="Illustrative cream and olive carton revisions separated on a bench beside a new artwork proof" width="1536" height="1024" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" class="w-full rounded-xl" /></figure></div></header>
<article class="container max-w-default prose-apex py-12 md:py-20">
<p>A new print file does not retire the cartons already in your warehouse. Before approving replacement artwork, decide exactly which orders may use the old version, what happens to unused stock, and who can release the new version to packing. Those decisions connect design approval to purchasing and fulfilment.</p>
<p>This guide addresses a planned commercial artwork change for Canadian and US business buyers. If the change corrects inaccurate product information or a safety issue, do not assume existing packaging can be used up: have the responsible product owner determine its permitted disposition first. The inventory method below never overrides that decision.</p>
<h2>Choose the switching rule before choosing the order quantity</h2>
<p>There are three useful starting options. A run-out transition allows approved old packaging to be consumed before the replacement enters use. A fixed cutover assigns the new version to orders from a defined point, even if old stock remains. A customer-specific transition assigns versions by account or channel. Pick one rule per SKU and destination; an instruction such as “use the new boxes when they arrive” leaves receiving staff to make a commercial decision.</p>
<p>A run-out can suit a cosmetic refresh where customers accept either version. A fixed cutover can suit a coordinated launch. Customer-specific transitions may be needed when a retailer has approved one presentation while another account accepts the other. Record these permissions explicitly. Do not infer them from the fact that the product itself is unchanged.</p>
<h2>Build a stock ledger that includes the supplier</h2>
<p>Count usable old blanks at your warehouse, at the packer and at any overflow location. Add quantities already committed in production or transit, but keep them in separate columns until their status is confirmed. Subtract allocations to existing orders only once. Finished goods in old packaging belong in a separate ledger: they affect customer-facing changeover timing but cannot supply empty cartons to the packing line.</p>
<p>Ask the supplier to identify open purchase orders by artwork revision. A purchase order with the same item number as last month can accidentally authorize another run of the old print. Obtain written agreement on whether unstarted work can be stopped, whether printed work must be accepted, and which costs apply. Cancellation rights, storage arrangements and credits depend on the agreed project terms.</p>
<h2>A run-out calculation with an explicit decision point</h2>
<p>Consider an invented stationery buyer with 4,800 usable old cartons on hand and 1,200 already printed cartons due to arrive. There are 900 units allocated to orders that must retain the old design and 300 units reserved for agreed service replacements. That leaves <strong>4,800 + 1,200 − 900 − 300 = 4,800 cartons</strong> available for ordinary run-out orders, assuming the incoming stock passes receipt checks.</p>
<p>At 800 cartons consumed per week, the ordinary balance covers six weeks. At 1,200 per week, it covers four. This is a consumption scenario, not a production or freight promise. Request a confirmed arrival plan for the replacement cartons and include inspection and release time before treating them as available.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Changeover decision comparison" tabindex="0"><table><caption>Illustrative choices if the launch is four weeks away</caption><thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>At 800 cartons per week</th><th>Decision to document</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Use approved old stock until exhausted</td><td>1,600 ordinary-use cartons remain at launch</td><td>Allow the old presentation beyond launch or move the launch date</td></tr>
<tr><td>Switch all ordinary orders at launch</td><td>1,600 cartons need a disposition decision</td><td>Hold, authorized rework, or disposal; identify the owner and cost</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reserve old stock for an approved account</td><td>The balance stays available only to that account</td><td>Create a separate allocation and prevent general picking</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The 900 allocated cartons and 300 service cartons remain excluded from the ordinary balance throughout this example. Review the ledger again immediately before cutover. If receipts, consumption or permissions change, update the decision instead of silently borrowing from protected stock.</p>
<h2>Give each revision an identity beyond its colour</h2>
<p>Use a controlled artwork filename and revision identifier on the purchase order, approved proof, retained sample and receiving record. Keep the packaging component code distinct from the finished-product identifier. Two versions can look almost identical from the outside of a bundle, and colour alone is an unreliable picking instruction.</p>
<p>Barcode decisions require a separate review. The <a href="https://ref.gs1.org/standards/gtin-management/">GS1 GTIN Management Standard</a> requires a new GTIN when the primary brand changes, with changes also required at existing packaging hierarchy levels above the retail base unit. Review the applicable rules for the actual product change rather than automatically retaining or replacing the barcode because the artwork file has a new revision. Have the brand owner record that decision before print approval.</p>
<p>If a new code is required, synchronize the product record, case information and customer setup with the physical change. The <a href="/blog/barcode-verification-custom-retail-boxes">retail-box barcode verification guide</a> covers the print checks that follow; a readable symbol does not by itself prove that it identifies the intended version.</p>
<figure><img src="/images/blog/2026-11-26/revision-receiving-inspection.webp" alt="Illustrative navy cartons checked against a retained sample, with rust-coloured cartons separated in a hold tray" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" /></figure>
<h2>Make receiving and line clearance part of the purchase</h2>
<p>Request revision identification on bundles and shipment documents, and ask whether different revisions will ever share a pallet. Agree the handling method before dispatch. At receipt, compare the delivered print with the released reference, reconcile counts by revision and segregate discrepancies. Stock marked “received” should not automatically become stock marked “approved for packing.”</p>
<p>At the changeover, remove remaining old blanks, loose sleeves, inserts and labels from the station. Check nearby replenishment bins as well as the machine or bench. Record the last order packed with the previous version and the first order packed with the replacement. A supervisor should authorize restart against the chosen switching rule and a complete first pack, not a loose carton alone.</p>
<p>Where multiple components change together, create a compatibility list: carton revision, insert revision, product version and any enclosed literature. An approved new carton around an outdated insert is still an uncontrolled combination. Use the <a href="/blog/corrugated-box-sample-approval-checklist">sample approval checks</a> for fit and closure when the change also affects structure or packout.</p>
<h2>Approval checklist: assign a name to each release</h2>
<ul><li>The product owner has specified which old-version stock remains usable and for which orders.</li><li>Purchasing has reconciled warehouse, packer, transit and supplier commitments by revision.</li><li>The brand owner has approved artwork and recorded the GTIN decision.</li><li>The supplier has acknowledged the replacement revision and the treatment of open orders.</li><li>Receiving has a reference sample, segregation location and release authority.</li><li>Operations has a line-clearance record and a first-pack approval requirement.</li><li>The commercial owner has authorized the residual-stock plan and its costs.</li></ul>
<h2>Ask for a changeover quotation, not just a replacement print run</h2>
<p>Send the current and proposed artwork identifiers, usable stock counts, open orders, forecast by week, account restrictions and intended switch date. Ask for separate confirmation of production status, available quantity options, artwork or tooling charges, shipment identification and the proposed delivery sequence. A lower unit price is not a saving if the order creates stock that cannot be used after cutover.</p>
